    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right about APA 7th edition. When citing a source with 3 or more authors, you'll use the \"et al.\" abbreviation from the first time you cite it, and then just use the year of publication in subsequent citations. So, if you have a source with 5 authors, your in-text citation would be (Author1 et al., 2020), and then you wouldn't need to list all the authors' names again in subsequent citations.\n\nNow, about Chicago. In the 17th edition of the Chicago Manual of Style, when citing a source with multiple authors, you'll use the \"author-date\" format. However, if you have 5 or more authors, you'll need to use a \"shortened\" author list, where you only include the first author's name, followed by \"et al.\" and the year of publication. So, in this case, your in-text citation would be (Author1 et al., 2020).",
